# `#[service]`

`#[service]` turns a struct into an RPC service: a shared singleton component with service metadata and a registry into which `#[handlers]` contributes RPC descriptors. Use the macro through the facade, `upwell::daemon`; do not import `upwell_rpc_macros` directly in application code.

<SymbolMeta />
<SymbolSignature />

```rust
use upwell::daemon::{handlers, rpc, service};

#[service]
struct Catalog;

#[handlers]
impl Catalog {
    #[rpc]
    async fn health(&self) -> &'static str {
        "ok"
    }
}
```

## Syntax

Apply the attribute to a struct item. It accepts the normal component arguments as well as the RPC-specific arguments below.

```rust
#[service(
    id = "billing",
    version = "1.2",
    rpc_slice = BillingMethods,
)]
struct BillingService;
```

| Argument | Accepted value | Default |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Component arguments | The ordinary component identity and construction arguments accepted by the component macro. | Component defaults. |
| `version` | String literal service version. | No version in service metadata. |
| `rpc_slice` | Identifier naming the service's link-time RPC slice. | `{Service}Rpcs`. |

The component arguments determine the generated component identity in the usual way. `#[service]` fixes the service scope to a shared singleton; an explicitly non-singleton scope is rejected.

## Generated artifacts

For a native target, expansion emits the normal singleton component metadata plus:

| Artifact | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| Service descriptor | Identifies the service, carries its component/service metadata and exposes its collected RPC methods. |
| `{Service}Rpcs` link-time slice | The default registry receiving descriptors emitted by `#[handlers]` blocks. `rpc_slice` changes its name. |
| Service registration glue | Makes the descriptor discoverable by the daemon/RPC protocol builder. |
| `{Service}Client<C>` | Generated only with the RPC macro crate's `client` feature; it has typed methods for marked RPCs. |

The service macro does not itself mount a transport endpoint or instantiate a server. Register or auto-discover the service through the daemon protocol/application configuration.

## Containment and composition

`#[service]` belongs on the service struct, while RPC methods belong in one or more inherent `#[handlers] impl Service` blocks. The blocks may be separated by concern, but they must target this service and contribute to the same default registry unless a matching `rpc_slice` is selected.

```rust
use upwell::daemon::{handlers, rpc, service};

#[service(rpc_slice = CatalogMethods)]
struct Catalog;

#[handlers(rpc_slice = CatalogMethods)]
impl Catalog {
    #[rpc]
    async fn lookup(&self) -> String {
        String::from("catalog")
    }
}
```

The type can also have lifecycle methods supplied by the component facilities and a separate `#[jobs]` implementation for scheduled work. Keep RPC handlers and scheduled jobs in separate inherent implementations so each collecting macro owns its marker attributes.

## Features and errors

Use the facade feature that enables daemon/RPC support. Native builds receive service and descriptor registration; generated clients are separately gated by the macro crate's `client` feature.

Compilation fails when the target is not a supported struct, when component arguments request a non-singleton scope, or when an RPC handler is attached to a type that is not declared as a service. A registry mismatch between `rpc_slice` settings leaves handlers unable to contribute to the intended service registry; use the same identifier on the service and every customized handler block.
